it may have been a home where children  once played or perhaps a roadside  business that helped parents put food on  the table maybe a guest house for  passing tourists  whatever it was it is now and forever  part of a massive debris field that runs  the length of the malamchi river in  sindhupalchak district  in valleys across nepal homes and  businesses have been swallowed up by  major floods  three times this year  weeks on from the latest delusion  october the number of dead and missing  is still rising and the list of damaged  and destroyed buildings is getting  longer  many nepalese have been living in limbo  for months  [Music]  is a mother and a grandmother she and  her husband invested everything they had  in trout farms that sat at the river's  edge  a trade they had hoped to pass on to the  next generation what they spent years  building was gone in a day  my son and husband were trying to save  the farm but then we had to run as we  were watching flood waters came and  swept away all the trees and we took the  kids and escaped the next morning we  came back to have a look and everything  was gone  her daughter bema ran the family cafe  which also became a casualty of  unseasonably extreme weather she says  she now feels lost  i could not have even imagined what  happened we sacrificed a lot to build  that place and it didn't even last two  years i'm very sad about that  thousands of people across nepal just  like bhima have lost everything and are  trying to rebuild their lives from  scratch  families displaced by the floods are  living in these makeshift camps for now  but they're waiting for more  semi-permanent housing to be built  further uphill now that's going to be  made out of corrugated metal sheets not  necessarily the best protection against  the coming winter cold but even though  the river has receded the idea right now  is to get as far away from the water  line as possible and onto more solid  ground but even uphill that may not be  so easy to come by disaster management  experts are warning that areas in the  himalayan region that saw heavy rains  are now at greater risk from landslides  zain basravi al-jazeera sindhu palchak  district nepal
 

